Fourth, no key state of a cipher or a connection stays behind in freed
or vacated memory. aes 0.8.4's autodetecting Aes256 is a union of the
hardware (AES-NI, or ARMv8 with aes_armv8) and the software key
schedules: new writes and Drop wipes only the one it selected, so the
rest of the union keeps the stack bytes it was built over, which were
seen to hold proxy secrets and derived obfuscation keys (on x86_64 the
unused 480-byte half of the 960-byte union). AesCtr and AesIge now hold
their Aes256 in a private mtproto-core holder, in safe code: on drop it
drops the cipher where it lies, then zeroizes a byte array laid over
all of the cipher's space (a repr(u8) enum whose other variant is that
array). zeroize's Option::zeroize is not used: it ends by writing a None
copied from a stack temporary, which puts stack bytes back over the
wiped space. Aes256::new still selects the backend, so AES-NI, ARMv8 and
the software one stay where they were. And every Connection is boxed:
Connection::connect, over_carrier and over_websocket give a
Box<Connection>, which a session's connection and racer slots and its
WebSocket probes keep, so closing a connection, promoting a racer,
adopting a WebSocket probe and moving a session in the worker's table
(a rehash, or its removal when the session is destroyed) move a
pointer, and the connection's cipher, keystream and fake-TLS state (the
proxy key) wipe themselves where they lie before their block is freed.
Before, the vacated slot, or the table bucket a destroyed session left,
kept their image until the table was freed. The test server records the
obfuscation header of each connection without fake TLS
(Stats::obfuscation_headers), from which a test derives the client's
keys, and security/requirements.tsv row H-03 follows. The five scanner
tests gated off on Windows x86_64 for the cipher's unused half run there
again; a new one requires the hardware key schedule in a live AesCtr
exactly where the CPU has AES (x86 and x86_64), and another closes a
session's connection by a pause, by destroying the session and during
a fake-TLS handshake, frees the session table and finds neither the
derived obfuscation keys nor the proxy key. Tests:
