Fifth, the engine reports its own panics to the host before the process
ends. A thread the engine starts runs as engine code for its whole
life, and every mt_* entry point (47, mt_set_panic_callback, the sixth
change's two and the eighth change's one included) begins with one
line, let _engine = enter_engine_code();, which runs the calling thread
as engine code for the whole call. mtproto-engine exports
enter_engine_code and report_panics_to; the first reporter set
installs, once, a panic hook chained in front of the one already set,
and until then nothing changes. For a panic of engine code the hook
calls the reporter on the panicking thread, before any unwinding, with
the thread's name (mtproto-main, mtproto-worker-N or mtproto-resolver;
empty for a thread the engine did not start) and
"<message> at <file>:<line>:<column>", then always calls the previous
hook, and the panic ends the process as before. Panics outside the
engine's threads and mt_* calls, such as another Rust library's on a
host thread, are not reported and unwind as before.

ABI 7 (no struct layout changes; ABI 8 of the sixth change builds on it)
adds the host's side of it to mtproto_engine.h:
  typedef void (*MTPanicCallback)(void *context, MTString thread, MTString message);
  void mt_set_panic_callback(void *context, MTPanicCallback callback);
The callback is process-wide. It is called on the panicking thread (an
engine thread, or the caller's inside an mt_* call) before the process
ends, with the thread's name and the message, strings that live for the
call. The host may end the process there. If the callback returns, the
process still ends, though not always by an abort: a panic on an engine
thread aborts it (0xC0000409 on Windows 8 and later, which only WER
sees), and so does one inside an mt_* call, except when panics unwind
on Windows and nothing above the call catches the panic, as on a host's
main thread: there it is a C++ exception no frame handles (0xE06D7363),
which the process's unhandled-exception filter, such as a crash
handler's, takes before the call could abort. A later call replaces the
callback and its context; NULL removes it. The ABI version checks and
docs/swift-integration.md follow. Tests:
mtproto-engine engine_thread::tests::a_panic_on_an_engine_thread_is_reported_with_its_thread_name_before_the_abort,
