Seventh, the engine wipes the session setups a host hands it, and every
command it posts to its workers, before it frees them. A value built on
the host's thread, such as the session setup mt_session_create
converts, leaves bytes unused (a None field's payload, padding) that
keep what that thread's stack held there, a proxy key in one seen case,
and the engine freed every copy unwiped: the command carrying it in the
channel's blocks, the box it came in, and the session table holding the
runtime that kept it, whose growth and removals leave copies of their
own. Now every command reaches its worker in a box of its own, so the
channel moves a pointer; the session setup, PFS setup, offered
temporary key, environment and web endpoint a command carries each have
a box of their own; and each session runtime lives boxed in the table,
so a rehash or a removal moves a pointer. Each of these boxes keeps its
value in a private holder that, in safe code, drops the value where it
lies or hands it to the worker that takes it out, and then zeroizes a
byte array laid over all of its space (a repr(u8) enum, as the cipher
holder of the fourth change). The address lists a host gives are
covered by the ninth change. No C ABI change. Tests:
mtproto-ffi sec_rows_zeroize::a_session_created_through_the_c_abi_leaves_no_host_stack_bytes_in_freed_memory,
mtproto-ffi sec_rows_zeroize::values_later_calls_hand_a_session_leave_no_host_stack_bytes_in_freed_memory.

Eighth, a host that resolves a proxy's host name itself, where the
system resolver may not answer it, gives a session the name's addresses
in the order to try them, and the session tries them in turn and keeps
its reconnect backoff across them. Before, such a host could only give
one of them as the proxy, and moving to the next, a different proxy,
started the backoff over. The addresses are used while the session's
proxy (SOCKS5, MTProxy or HTTP) has that name as its host, each at the
proxy's port, and kept across proxy changes, one name at a time, so the
proxy and its list may come in either order. While the session holds
them it never looks the name up, for a TCP connection, an HTTP link or
an Auto HTTP probe: each failed attempt goes to the next address while
the failure count, and with it the reconnect delay, keeps growing, as
for a name the engine resolves itself, so the delays and the proxy
connection issues are those of a single address, and after the last
address the next round starts over from the first, with no lookup, no
expiry and the count kept. An address a connection answered on goes
first from then on. A later list for the name takes over from the next
attempt and resets nothing (the failure count, the delay, the
connection in place); the same list again changes nothing. The first
list for a name lets a session without a connection try at once, as an
answer to its own lookup does. Entries that are not IP literals, and
repeats, are skipped; an empty list, or one without a literal, gives
the name back to the system resolver at its next lookup. A system
answer for the name that comes while a list is held, to a lookup asked
before it, is ignored: it counts no failure, moves no attempt and
installs no addresses. While connected through a proxy whose host is a
name, the session's EngineEvent::ConnectionState also names the address
of that name it reached: its TCP connection's, or that of an HTTP link
that answered. A proxy given as an IP literal, and a name without a
list, connect as before. A test drives three addresses that each fail
at once, and one such address, on a virtual clock, idle for five
minutes and with a call waiting for two, and finds the same delays and
as many attempts; it needs listeners on 127.0.0.2 to 127.0.0.4 and
skips, saying so, where they cannot be bound. Tests:
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_proxy_name_the_host_answers_for_rotates_with_the_failures_and_is_never_looked_up,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::the_backoff_keeps_growing_across_the_proxy_addresses_the_host_gives,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_new_list_for_the_proxy_name_keeps_the_backoff_and_the_connection,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_system_answer_for_a_name_the_host_answers_for_changes_nothing,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::http_through_a_named_proxy_uses_the_hosts_addresses,
mtproto-engine proxy_addresses::an_address_that_starts_accepting_carries_the_session_at_its_next_turn,
mtproto-engine proxy_addresses::an_http_link_names_the_address_reached_and_an_ip_literal_proxy_names_none.

ABI 9 (no struct layout changes; ABI 10 of the eleventh change builds on
it) adds it to mtproto_engine.h:
  void mt_session_set_proxy_addresses(MTEngine *engine, MTSessionHandle session, MTString host, const MTString *addresses, size_t count);
and text2 of MTEventKindConnectionState (18), empty before: while
connected through a proxy whose host is a name, the address of that
name the session reached, as an IP literal without the port, and empty
otherwise; over HTTP it comes once a link answered, so a state that
says connected may come first with text2 empty. text stays the proxy as
host:port. host is the proxy's host name and addresses its count
addresses as text, count 0 for an empty list; the strings are copied
during the call and the addresses parsed on the session's worker, not
on the host's thread, and the seventh change's test of later calls
hands a session this one too. A null engine is ignored. The ABI version
checks and docs/swift-integration.md follow. Tests:
mtproto-ffi ffi_abi9::the_header_declares_abi_9,
mtproto-ffi ffi_abi9::a_named_proxy_connects_only_through_the_addresses_the_host_gives.

Ninth, the engine wipes the address lists a host hands it before it
frees them. mt_session_create, mt_session_set_addresses and
mt_session_set_http_addresses build each list on the host's thread,
where an address without a secret leaves its None's payload and its
padding unused (16 and 6 bytes on x86_64, 8 and 2 on i686), keeping
what that thread's stack held there, and the engine freed every list
unwiped: an equal one at once, the one a new list replaced, one for a
session already gone, and the last when the session went. Now, wherever
the engine drops such a list, it drops the addresses where they lie and
then zeroes the whole buffer, in safe code: zeroize writes every byte
of the emptied list's spare capacity with a volatile one-byte write. A
session setup does so for its lists whenever it is dropped (with its
session, or with a command never carried out), and the two address
commands carry their list in a holder that does so, unused or holding
the list it replaced. The engine never grows or shrinks a list it
keeps, so no reallocation frees a buffer unwiped. This changes
mtproto-engine's public Rust API: SessionSetup implements Drop, so a
field can no longer be moved out of it, nor can a setup be the base of
struct update syntax (take or clone the field instead). No C ABI
change. Not covered: a Verification from mt_session_resolve_recaptcha
kept in a request's state, and the unused bytes of queued requests (at
most 8 in a row). Tests:
mtproto-ffi sec_rows_zeroize::address_lists_a_host_hands_a_session_leave_no_host_stack_bytes_in_freed_memory.

Tenth, the test server counts a client's reset apart from its clean
close, and paused_sessions_do_not_connect_until_resumed waits for the
paused session's close instead of sleeping 200 ms. With its call the
session asks for a pong and future salts; when the host pauses it right
after the answer they may still be unread, and Windows resets a
connection closed with data unread or still arriving. The test server
did not count a reset as the client's close, so the test failed now and
then on Windows (in 11 of 400 runs on x86_64, and in 9 of 400 in a
later series). Stats::reset_by_client counts the connections that ended
with ConnectionReset, ConnectionAborted or BrokenPipe while the server
read or answered the client's frames, closed_by_client still counts the
clean ends of stream only, and the test waits up to 10 s for either: a
session that ignores the pause fails it. The engine's pause does not
change: in each of 400 traced runs it closed the connection within
0.32 ms of the pause. Tests:
mtproto-engine engine::paused_sessions_do_not_connect_until_resumed.

Eleventh, the engine tells the host each transport error code a
connection gets. A session holding a key reported -444 and -403 alike,
as a ConnectionDropped with AddressRejected, and a code reached the
host only as text in AuthKeyCreationFailed during a handshake, so a
host could not answer a -444 with its own prompt about a bad proxy, as
Telegram Desktop does. Now EngineEvent::TransportError gives the code
first thing in on_transport_error_with, as it came in a short frame on
a connection or as its negative for the HTTP statuses that function
takes, before anything the code leads to, and what the session does
about the code does not change. A status of a plain HTTP request with
no handshake running, -404 on an HTTP route not yet proven, route
