Twelfth, a host can set a fake-TLS hello's time apart from the
session's, and learns when a dropped connection's hello never verified.
The hello took its time from the session's time difference, and the one
setter there is, set_time_difference, also moves the RPC session's
offset and future msg ids, where a forward shift leaves a monotonic
floor. An ee proxy refuses a hello outside its window, so a session
behind a wrong clock never reached a server to learn the time from one.
Now SessionRuntime holds an optional offset, None until the host gives
one, read only by the one calculation all five construction sites take:
the wall clock plus the offset, else the time difference. Setting or
clearing it is a store: no msg id, time difference, report, connection
or delay moves, a hello already constructed stays, and it follows no
correction. ConnectionDropped also says the connection was to carry a
fake-TLS hello and no ServerHello verified on it, from the transport's
own state, so it is true on a connection that never completed and on a
masked refusal as on a close, and false without an ee secret, on HTTP
links and once a hello verified. The test server can refuse a hello
outside a time window and records each hello's time
(ServerOptions::tls_time_window, Stats::tls_hello_times,
Stats::tls_time_refusals). Tests:
