Thirteenth, a session asks the server about a query that went ten
seconds without an answer on a connection that stays up. Only a closed
connection's queries were asked about or sent again, while the liveness
checks watch the link, which pings and the other answers keep alive, so
a query whose copy was lost on the way, or whose answer the server sent
on another connection of the session that then closed, waited for good:
an upload part did, for over five minutes, after an outage. Now each
query still without an answer, acknowledged or not, gets msgs_state_req
after ANSWER_WAIT (ten seconds, as Telegram Desktop's
checkSentRequests), again each time it waits that long more, also after
a reconnect, but not while large packets may still be crossing the
uplink, and not while a request is out for an answer the server
announced for it. When such a request goes unanswered for
TCP_ASK_BACKSTOP (its reply lost, or sent on another connection that
closed) the query is asked about again, and the server saying the
answer exists (+64) asks for that answer again: the reply comes behind
anything the server still sends on the connection, the answer included.
An announced answer the server no longer has fails the query once with
PROTOCOL_ERROR_ANSWER_LOST, never sent again: the server ran it, so a
host must not send it again either. As the server reads a connection in
order, and a closed connection's packets came before the question, a
reply to a request that went out after the query's latest copy speaks
of that copy. Not received sends it again under its msg_id while the
server still takes it, else under a new msg_id (a draining session
releases it as not run). Nothing known, which a server that ran the
query and forgot its msg_id also says, sends it again only under its
msg_id; past RETRANSMIT_WINDOW, after the server refused that copy, or
for a query the server acknowledged and no longer has, the query fails
once as possibly run with PROTOCOL_ERROR_QUERY_FORGOTTEN and code 0,
which is no server error for a host to retry (a draining session
releases it as possibly run), and never goes under a new msg_id, which
would run it a second time. Received with its answer made (+64) sends
it again under its msg_id to fetch that answer, which the server
answers from what it has without running it twice, each time the answer
stays away; received and still running, or not started, waits. A reply
about an earlier copy sends nothing again. Over HTTP nothing is asked,
and a reply there gives a query the server may have run no new msg_id
either. The test server reports +32 and +64 as Telegram's servers do,
can lose a query once on a live connection (TAG_SWALLOW_ONCE,
Stats::swallowed) and keep an answer off the connection its query came
on (TAG_ANSWER_ELSEWHERE_ONCE, Stats::answers_elsewhere). No C ABI
change. Tests:
