Fourteenth, a host can check the route a session would take to a
datacenter, through a proxy or without one, with one unauthenticated
request outside any session, as Telegram Desktop checks each proxy in
its list. Engine::start_route_check takes a RouteCheckSetup: the
datacenter address a SOCKS5 or HTTP proxy tunnels to (or the one
connected to without a proxy), the proxy, the addresses the host
resolved for the proxy's name, the framing, the obfuscation id, the
time difference for the request's msg id, an optional fake-TLS hello
offset and each attempt's bound, ROUTE_CHECK_TIMEOUT (10 s) for one not
finite and positive. Its handle comes from the sessions' counter, so no
session has it, and the check runs on a worker as a session other than
the main one would, in a map of its own with one token block, which no
session command reads. Each attempt opens one connection by the route
through Connection::connect, with the sessions' own tunnel, secret and
hello time calculations, now functions the sessions call (bodies
unchanged: the five hello construction sites keep their one
calculation and the check is its sixth caller), and sends one
req_pq_multi, the racer check's packet, once the connection is ready,
the tunnel made and a fake-TLS ServerHello verified. Only a whole
res_pq with the nonce it sent answers, read as the key handshake reads
one (mtproto_core's ResPq, its fingerprint vector bounded), so a reply
cut short or malformed answers nothing; any other packet, a transport
error code, a close, an error or the bound fails the attempt, which
moves to the next address, and the check ends with the last. A name
with addresses given is not looked up, any other goes to the worker's
resolver, and the lookup counts against the first attempt's bound.
That resolver runs at most four blocking lookups at once per worker
(MAX_LOOKUPS), each keeping its place until it returns, and queues the
rest oldest first, dropping a queued one before it starts once no
session or check waits for it: a long list of named proxies cannot
start a thread per name. EngineEvent::RouteChecked comes once per check
started: answered, with the seconds from the connection being ready to
the answer, or not, with how the last attempt ended (closed, io_error,
protocol, timeout, connect_failed, unresolved, wrong_answer or
transport_error), and the attempts made. Engine::cancel_route_check
ends a check with no event and closes its connection. The check holds
no key, no RPC client, no handshake and no environment: it can make or
use no key, sends no encrypted packet and no initConnection, asks no
route admission, opens no host stream, reports nothing to the route
hints and does not follow set_network_available, and session calls
with its handle reach no session (send fails the request with
SESSION_CLOSED). A WEB proxy starts no check. RouteCheckSetup wipes
its list of proxy addresses before it is freed, as a session setup
wipes its lists, so it implements Drop. The check is unrelated to the
HTTP link's own route check. The test server counts plain req_pq_multi
packets apart from the later handshake steps (Stats::req_pqs,
Stats::dh_requests). Tests:
mtproto-engine route_check::a_check_answers_directly_and_through_each_proxy_kind_with_one_unauthenticated_request,
mtproto-engine route_check::a_check_never_asks_for_or_makes_a_key_and_session_calls_do_not_reach_it,
